          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} mathematical structure
            whose traditional
            = succeed "-style" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            can be formulated
            %em internally
            (
            %a{:href => "#Grothendieck60"}> Grothendieck 1960
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Grothendieck61"} 61
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            that admits all those types of operations (typically:
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            that the traditional definition applies to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} base
            category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
          %p
            This
            %em internalization
            serves to combine mathematical structures in a compatible way. For example a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            internal to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            (while internal to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
            it is just a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete group
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagrammatic
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntactic
            way of systematizing the process of internalization: The former is essentially the topic of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+algebra"} categorical algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere63"}> Lawvere 1963
            ) or, more generally, of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sketches"} sketches
            (
            %a{:href => "#BastianiEhresmann72"}> Bastiani &amp; Ehresmann 1972
            ), the latter of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theories"} type theories
          %p
            An archetypical example of internalization is the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groups"} internal groups
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#EckmannHilton61"} Eckmann &amp; Hilton 1961
          %p
            Where the
            = succeed "-style" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            is, of course:
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %em group
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              equipped with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} e
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{e} \,\colon\,\ast \to G
              (the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutral+element"} neutral element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \colon G \times G \to G
              (the group
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+operation"} operation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^{-1} \colon G \to G
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+elements"}> inverse elements
              ) satisfying three conditions:
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unitality
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associativity
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverses"} inverses
          %p one observes that here:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
            %li
              %p
                in invoking the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton+set"} singleton set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
                and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
                set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo ×
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \times G
                one is making use of the fact that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
                admit the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal construction
                of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
          %p
            and that this is
            %em only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} structure
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
            that the definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            is making use of. Therefore, we may abstract away from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
            %em any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
            and declare that:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+group"} internal group
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                equipped with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
