          %p
            If here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
            then this recovers the
            = succeed "-definition" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bourbaki"} Bourbaki
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TopologicalSpaces"} TopologicalSpaces
            this yields the notion of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groups"} topological groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothManifolds"} SmoothManifolds
            it yields
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Varieties"} Varieties
            it yields
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+groups"} algebraic groups
          %p
            Similarly one defines
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+objects"} internal actions
            of internal groups,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+principal+actions"} formally principal actions
          %p
            In fact, realizing that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            are equivalently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-connected+object+of+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            one may readily generalize the example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groups"} internal groups
            to a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoids"} internal groupoids
            and then of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and with it much of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            may be considered internally to suitable ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            (see also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
          %p
            Moreover, if the ambient category is in fact a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+group"} internal group
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Groupoids"} Groupoids
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-group"} strict 2-group
            if one considers just the three
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
            shown above, or a general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
            of one adds higher
            = succeed "-diagrams.)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence"} coherence
          %p
            How much mathematics/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            actually exists in a given ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            depends on how much
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+properties"} extra properties
            this has. For example if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            then
            %em all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            has an internal incarnation in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %p
            Here is a list matching some extra structure/property of the ambient category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            to the kind of mathematics that exists internal to it:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                If the ambient category is equipped with the structure of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                locally on the domain with patching conditions (or more generally
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
                theory), exist inside it.
            %li
              %p
                If it is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+objects"} terminal objects
                means that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety+of+algebras"} varieties of algebras
                can be defined.
            %li
              %p
                If the category is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                with a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} impredicative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
                without the fancier tools of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+theory"} model theory
                or (ironically)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %li
              %p
                Further extra conditions on the category, such as being a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
                or being a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superextensive+site"} superextensive site
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %li
              %p
                If the category is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} (infinity,1)-topos
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+excluded+middle"} principle of excluded middle
                or the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom
                of
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/choice+object"} choice set
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-groupoids"} 0-groupoids
                results in internal classical mathematics.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            required on the ambient category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is sometimes referred to as a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
            for internalization. For example, there is a doctrine of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} categories with finite limits
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+categories"} cartesian closed categories
          %p#Externalization
            Like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} categorification
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+categorification"} oidification
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            to which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets"} Sets
            (or some canonical Set-like category) belongs and a definition of foo internalized in the doctrine
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sets
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sets
            reduces to the usual definition of foo, then the definition is acceptable; foos are a
            = succeed "," do
              %em deinternalization
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/externalization"} externalization
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} Monoids
                can be internalized in the doctrine of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal categories
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+objects"}> monoid objects
                ). For example:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    A
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                    is a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
                    in
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
                    with its usual tensor product.
                %li
                  %p
                    An
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
                    is a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
                    internal to
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
                %li
                  %p
                    A strict monoidal category is a monoid internal to
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %li
              %p
                Since any category with finite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} products
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} Groups
                can also be internalized in the doctrine of categories with finite products, but not in the doctrine of monoidal categories, since diagonal maps are necessary to define what it means to have inverses.
            %li
              %p
                Monoids can also be internalized in the doctrine of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategories
            %li
              %p
                Commutative monoids can be internalized in the doctrine of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal categories
            %li
              %p
                Categories and groupoids can be internalized in the doctrine of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} lex categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal category
              %ul
                %li
                  The classical fact that monoids can be identified with one-object categories has the following internal analogue: monoids in a lex category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  (qua category with finite products) can be identified with categories internal to
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  whose object-of-objects is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ab
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab
                  or
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Vect
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} Rings
                can be internalized in the doctrine of categories with finite products.
            %li
              %p
                More generally, the algebras for any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
                can be internalized in the doctrine of categories with finite products, and the algebras for any (symmetric)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
                (in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %li
              %p
                Pretty much any structure at all in mathematics can be internalized in a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                of a topos is constructive, differences in axiomatization that make no difference classically can result in actual differences in behavior in a topos. See
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
                for some examples. On the other hand, if the topos satisfies the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                (and in particular is
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean
            %li
              %p
                Categories themselves can be internalised, as algebras of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} essentially algebraic theory
                (giving
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal category
          %h2#general_results General Results
          %p
            Often, the structure on the ambient category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            allowing a certain type of structure to be internalized in it is itself a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} categorified
            version of that same structure (for example, monoids in monoidal categories). This is an example of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/12/the_microcosm_principle.html"} microcosm principle
          %p
            As one formal result along these lines,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"} Tom Leinster
            has shown that for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monad"} cartesian monad
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-multicategories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-structured" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is the monad whose algebras are monoids, this says that monoids can be internalized in multicategories and monoidal categories.
          %p
            On the other hand, this is not always true. For example, the categorification of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig"} rig
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+category"} rig category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            does not use the rig-category structure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            but only that it has finite products.
          %p
            A very different sort of general result has to do with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of certain categories. If a category has enough structure to interpret a certain fragment of first-order logic, then any first-order theory definable in that fragment can be internalized in that category. For example, the theory of categories can be formulated in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+logic"} cartesian logic
            and thus is interpretable in any cartesian (= lex) category. The statement that almost anything can be internalized in a topos is the high-end case of this, since the internal logic of a topos is full intuitionistic type theory.
          %h2#examples_2 Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+object"} action object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-torsor"} pseudo-torsor object
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
                = succeed "-object)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+object"} ring object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoid object in an (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+object"} Lie algebra object
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+crossed+module"} internal crossed module
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+locale"} internal locale
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal category
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+diagram"} internal diagram
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+site"}> internal site
                    ,
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoid"} internal groupoid
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+pseudocategory"} internal pseudocategory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"}> groupoid object in an (∞,1)-category
                    ,
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} category object in an (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+%28co-%29limit"} internal (co-)limit
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
          %h2#References References
          %p The general notion of internalization is due to
          %ul
            %li#Grothendieck60
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              , p. 370 (3 of 23) in:
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FGA"} FGA
              = succeed "," do
                %em Technique de descente et théorèmes d’existence en géométrie algébriques. II: Le théorème d’existence en théorie formelle des modules
              %strong 195
              (1960) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/SB_1958-1960__5__369_0"}> numdam:SB_1958-1960__5__369_0
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/SB_1958-1960__5__369_0.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://translations.thosgood.com/fga/fga3.ii.xml"} web version
          %p
            with specialization to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groups"} internal groups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+actions"} internal actions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoids"} internal groupoids
            made explicit in:
          %ul
            %li#Grothendieck61
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              , Sections 3, 4 of:
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FGA"} FGA
              = succeed "," do
                %em Techniques de construction et théorèmes d’existence en géométrie algébrique III: préschémas quotients
              %strong 212
              (1961) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=SB_1960-1961__6__99_0"}> numdam:SB_1960-1961__6__99_0
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/SB_1960-1961__6__99_0.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://translations.thosgood.com/fga/fga3.iii.xml"} web version
          %p
            and to the case of internal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
            (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-torsor"} pseudo-torsor
          %ul
            %li#Grothendieck60
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
              , p. 312 (15 of 30) in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Technique de descente et théorèmes d’existence en géométrie algébrique. I. Généralités. Descente par morphismes fidèlement plats
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=SB_1958-1960__5__299_0"} numdam:SB_1958-1960__5__299_0
              = succeed ");" do
                %a{:href => "https://translations.thosgood.com/fga/fga3.i.xml"} web version
          %p
            Discussion with focus on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H-spaces"} H-spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+monoids"} internal monoids
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groups"} internal groups
            and proving the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+argument"} Eckmann-Hilton argument
            originates with:
          %ul
            %li#EckmannHilton61
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beno+Eckmann"}> Beno Eckmann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Hilton"} Peter Hilton
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Structure maps in group theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4064/fm-50-2-207-221"} doi:10.4064/fm-50-2-207-221
            %li#EckmannHilton62
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beno+Eckmann"}> Beno Eckmann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Hilton"} Peter Hilton
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Group-like structures in general categories I. Multiplications and comultiplications
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01451367"} doi:10.1007/BF01451367
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beno+Eckmann"}> Beno Eckmann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+J.+Hilton"} Peter J. Hilton
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Group-like structures in general categories II. Equalizers, limits, lengths
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/bf01344176"} doi:10.1007/bf01344176
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beno+Eckmann"}> Beno Eckmann
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Hilton"} Peter Hilton
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Group-like structures in general categories III. Primitive categories
                %strong 150
                165–187 (1963) (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01470843"}> doi:10.1007/BF01470843
                )
          %p
            Highlighting the role of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            in internalization:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
              , Section III.6 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
          %p
            Moreover with discussion of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+objects"} action objects
          %ul
            %li#MacLaneMoerdijk92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4612-0927-0"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4612-0927-0
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Janelidze"} George Janelidze
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gregory+Maxwell+Kelly"} Gregory Maxwell Kelly
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Internal object actions
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/249553"} dml:249553
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            is often attributed to originate around (though the simple idea can hardly be recognized here):
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Ehresmann"}> Charles Ehresmann
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Catégories structurées
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/ASENS_1963_3_80_4_349_0"} numdam:ASENS_1963_3_80_4_349_0
          %p
            Review of internal category theory (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"}> internal categories
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+functors"} internal functors
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+limits"} internal limits
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+sites"} internal sites
          %ul
            %li#Johnstone77
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                , Chapter 2 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topos theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 10
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#MacLaneMoerdijk92"} MacLane-Moerdijk 92, Sec. V.7
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , Chapter 8 in Vol 1
                %em Basic Category Theory
                of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511525858"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511525858
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                , Chapter B2 in: Vol. 1 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant+--+A+Topos+Theory+Compendium"} Sketches of an Elephant – A Topos Theory Compendium
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://global.oup.com/academic/product/sketches-of-an-elephant-9780198534259"} ISBN:9780198534259
          %p
            Review of internal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+objects"} group objects
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+objects"} action objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            (though without attribution to the above original articles):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Michael+Boardman"}> John Michael Boardman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic objects in categories
                %em Stable Operations in Generalized Cohomology
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://hopf.math.purdue.edu/Boardman/stabop.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack; in:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ioan+Mackenzie+James"} Ioan Mackenzie James
                (ed.)
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Algebraic+Topology"} Handbook of Algebraic Topology
                Oxford (1995) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-444-81779-2.X5000-7"}> doi:10.1016/B978-0-444-81779-2.X5000-7
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Magnus+Forrester-Barker"}> Magnus Forrester-Barker
                ,
                %em Group Objects and Internal Categories
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0212065"}> arXiv:math/0212065
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            More general internal algebraic structures, known as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+Lawvere+theory"} algebras
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
            originate with:
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere63
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Functorial+Semantics+of+Algebraic+Theories"} Functorial Semantics of Algebraic Theories
              %strong 5
              (2004) pp 1–121. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/5/tr5abs.html"}> tac:tr5
              )
          %p
            Yet more general internal structures via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sketches"} sketches
          %ul
            %li#BastianiEhresmann72
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9e+Bastiani"}> Andrée Bastiani
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Ehresmann"} Charles Ehresmann
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categories of sketched structures
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=CTGDC_1972__13_2_104_0"} numdam:CTGDC_1972__13_2_104_0
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Wells"} Charles Wells
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toposes%2C+Triples%2C+and+Theories"} Toposes, Triples, and Theories
              %p reprinted in:
              %p
                Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories
                %strong 12
                (2005) 1-287 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/12/tr12abs.html"}> tac:tr12
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p For more see at:
          %ul
            %li
